## Lifecycle and Ownership

Creating, changing, and reading a provider requires a Redshift database superuser, because
[SVV_IDENTITY_PROVIDERS](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_SVV_IDENTITY_PROVIDERS.html) is visible only
to superusers.

Deletion never uses `CASCADE`, which would drop the provider's users and roles. It refuses to drop the provider while
namespace-prefixed federated users remain. Manage role grants and memberships separately; use resource dependencies so
they are removed first.

## Reconciliation

Refresh reads the type, namespace, enabled status, and the type's binding from `SVV_IDENTITY_PROVIDERS`: the
application ARN and IAM role for `awsidc`, and the issuer, client ID, and audience for `azure`. An update runs
`ALTER IDENTITY PROVIDER` statements for the changed settings, always reapplying the IAM role and the status, and
re-reads the catalog to verify the result; a new `name`, `type`, or `application_arn` replaces the provider. Creation
runs `CREATE IDENTITY PROVIDER`, followed by `ALTER IDENTITY PROVIDER ... DISABLE` when `enabled = false`, because a
new provider is always enabled.

Every update of an `awsidc` provider starts with `IAM_ROLE` and every update ends with `ENABLE` or `DISABLE`, whether
or not they changed, so an update also repairs a role or status changed outside Terraform since the last refresh.
`ALTER IDENTITY PROVIDER ... PARAMETERS` replaces every Azure parameter, so a change to any of them sends all of them
together with `client_secret_wo` from the configuration, which must then be set.

| Change                                                                                         | Result                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |
|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| `name`                                                                                         | replaces the identity provider: the provider never renames it                                                                                                                                                                                     |
| `type`                                                                                         | replaces the identity provider: the two types have different bindings; an omitted `type` is `awsidc`                                                                                                                                              |
| `application_arn`                                                                              | replaces the identity provider: `ALTER IDENTITY PROVIDER` cannot change the managed application                                                                                                                                                   |
| `namespace`                                                                                    | `ALTER IDENTITY PROVIDER ... NAMESPACE`, after `IAM_ROLE`; refused before any `ALTER` statement runs while users with the previous `namespace:` prefix exist, because Redshift keeps their names and deletion would no longer find them           |
| `iam_role_arn`                                                                                 | `ALTER IDENTITY PROVIDER ... IAM_ROLE`, the first statement of every update of an `awsidc` provider                                                                                                                                               |
| `issuer`, `client_id`, or `audience`                                                           | `ALTER IDENTITY PROVIDER ... PARAMETERS` with every Azure parameter and `client_secret_wo`; when `issuer` changes, the plan shows `instance_id` and `identity_center_instance_arn` as known after apply, because the instance ID holds the tenant |
| `client_secret_wo`                                                                             | no plan change and no SQL: write-only values are never compared; change `client_secret_wo_version` to send it                                                                                                                                     |
| `client_secret_wo_version`                                                                     | `ALTER IDENTITY PROVIDER ... PARAMETERS` with every Azure parameter and `client_secret_wo`; no `PARAMETERS` when it is set for the first time after an import, because the secret version in state is then null                                   |
| `auto_create_roles`, `auto_create_roles_include_groups`, or `auto_create_roles_exclude_groups` | `ALTER IDENTITY PROVIDER ... AUTO_CREATE_ROLES TRUE`, with `INCLUDE GROUPS LIKE` or `EXCLUDE GROUPS LIKE` for a filter, or `AUTO_CREATE_ROLES FALSE`, after `NAMESPACE` and `PARAMETERS`                                                          |
| `auto_create_roles` removed                                                                    | `AUTO_CREATE_ROLES` with the type's default, `FALSE` for `awsidc` and `TRUE` for `azure`; no SQL when the removed value was that default                                                                                                          |
| `enabled`                                                                                      | `ALTER IDENTITY PROVIDER ... ENABLE` or `DISABLE`, the last statement of every update; removing it sets the default `true`                                                                                                                        |

**Drift.** Refresh compares the catalog with the state, and the next plan restores the configuration with the
statements above: a changed `namespace`, `iam_role_arn`, `issuer`, `client_id`, `audience`, or `enabled` status is set
back, which for the Azure parameters needs `client_secret_wo`. A provider dropped outside Terraform is removed from
state and planned for creation. `auto_create_roles`, its group filters, `client_secret_wo_version`, and the client
secret are never read back, so changes to them outside Terraform are not detected.

The following examples change a provider and show the statements the update runs.

### Removing Automatic Role Creation

Removing `auto_create_roles` and its include filter from an `awsidc` provider restores the type's default, `FALSE`.
The IAM role and the status are reapplied around it:

```terraform
resource "redshift_identity_provider" "this" {
  name            = "identity"
  namespace       = "example"
  application_arn = "application"
  iam_role_arn    = "role-one"
  # Removed:
  # auto_create_roles                = true
  # auto_create_roles_include_groups = "finance_%"
}
```

{{ codefile "sql" "internal/provider/testdata/sql/identity_provider/alter_auto_create_roles_reset_awsidc.sql" }}

### Rotating the Azure Client Secret

A new `client_secret_wo_version` sends the secret from `client_secret_wo` together with the unchanged parameters:

```terraform
resource "redshift_identity_provider" "entra" {
  name                     = "oauth_standard"
  type                     = "azure"
  namespace                = "aad"
  issuer                   = "https://sts.windows.net/2sdfdsf-d475-420d-b5ac-667adad7c702/"
  client_id                = "87f4aa26-78b7-410e-bf29-57b39929ef9a"
  audience                 = ["https://analysis.windows.net/powerbi/connector/AmazonRedshift"]
  client_secret_wo         = ephemeral.aws_secretsmanager_secret_version.entra.secret_string
  client_secret_wo_version = 2 # was 1
}
```

{{ codefile "sql" "internal/provider/testdata/sql/identity_provider/alter_azure_rotate_secret.sql" }}

## Import

In Terraform 1.5 and later, use an `import` block with a JSON identity:

{{ tffile "examples/resources/redshift_identity_provider/import-by-string-id.tf" }}

The example binds a Serverless workgroup. Replace `workgroup_name` with `cluster_identifier` for a provisioned
cluster, or with `endpoint` (`host:port`) for a direct password connection; see
[connection methods](../guides/connection_methods.md#imports-and-transport-switching).

Alternatively, use `terraform import`:

{{ codefile "shell" "examples/resources/redshift_identity_provider/import.sh" }}

Refresh discovers the type, namespace, and type-specific binding and the enabled state. It cannot discover
`auto_create_roles` or the client secret, and an imported provider sends no secret when `client_secret_wo_version` is
first set. Match configuration to the observed identity before applying a plan.
